ANSWER: {"answer": "Child Benefit (gov.uk/child-benefit) - Eligibility ('Who can get Child Benefit'): Only one person can get Child Benefit per child. You normally qualify if responsible for a child under 16 and living in the UK; responsible means you live with them or pay at least the same amount as Child Benefit (or equivalent in kind) towards their care. If the child is 16+, you normally still qualify if under 20 and in approved education/training. Different rules apply for fostering, adopting, informal care of others' children, living abroad, and EU Settlement Scheme statuses; you stop getting it if the child starts 24+ hrs/week paid work while not in approved education, starts an apprenticeship in England, or claims certain benefits. How it works: You get Child Benefit if responsible for a child under 16 (or under 20 in approved education/training); no limit on number of children. It provides an allowance paid every 4 weeks, National Insurance credits towards your State Pension (automatic if child under 12), and a National Insurance number for your child. You must report any change of circumstances to the Child Benefit Office. How to claim ('Make a claim'): Claim 48 hours after registering the birth or once a child comes to live with you; can be backdated up to 3 months. Only one person can claim per child, so decide who claims (whoever claims gets the NI credits). Claim online, by post, or by phone. You need the child's birth/adoption certificate (if you have it), bank/building society details, your NI number, and your partner's NI number. The online claim starts via a 'Start now' button (would require signing in, so stopped there)."}

